    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Christian Yelich** (75.84) and **Yordan Alvarez** (71.11) headlining our offensive attack. **Yelich** is our premier play, benefiting from a favorable matchup against Sugano and a significant +13% dynamic platoon boost, while **Yordan Alvarez** continues to command an elite xwOBA (0.478) that keeps his floor remarkably high despite facing Martin. We are also leaning into the middle infield, where **Caleb Durbin** (65.95) and **Casey Schmitt** (63.92) provide excellent value. **Casey Schmitt** specifically benefits from the wind blowing out, providing a nice tailwind to a projection that already accounted for a soft matchup against Rodriguez.\n\nOn the margin, **Lars Nootbaar** (63.36) remains a strong start despite a 41% rain risk in his contest against Lowder. The most difficult personnel decision centered on **Carson Benge** (55.19), who narrowly missed the starting cut. While **Carson Benge** brings respectable utility, the aggregate efficiency of our current starters\u2014particularly the order-based upside for **Colt Keith** (58.81) and the stolen base potential of **Trea Turner** (53.92)\u2014pushed him to the bench. We are tracking **Trea Turner** closely; while his raw output remains high, his score is currently dampened by a tough matchup against the arm of Schlittler, making him a volatility point in our pursuit of category-winning speed.\n\nManagers should monitor the status of **Lars Nootbaar** closely throughout the afternoon; should the weather in his game deteriorate, we will look to rotate our bench depth. Otherwise, we are avoiding **Dillon Dingler** (41.99) where possible, as his score remains suppressed by a negative platoon split against Way. With **Hyeseong Kim** still stashed in the minors and **Nelson Velazquez** out of the lineup, our bench options are limited, so protecting our games-played cap while maintaining high-efficiency exposure remains the priority for today's matchup against this slate of arms.",
